    "case_id": "652",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old female with medical history of type 2 diabetes, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and nonischemic cardiomyopathy (ejection fraction 25% to 34%). She was receiving milrinone at home through a double lumen tunneled catheter. She had an implantable cardioverter defibrillator placed 5 years prior and had been disabled for 12 years. Four days before presentation, she developed cough and chills. Three days later, she experienced shortness of breath, weakness, increased lower extremity edema, and diarrhea.\n\nPatient was afebrile and hemodynamically stable with 98% oxygen saturation on room air. Examination revealed:- Jugular venous distension- Bilateral basilar pulmonary crackles on auscultation- Lower extremity edema- Tunneled catheter site showed no erythema, warmth, drainage, or tendernessLater developed:- Temperature of 39°C- Sinus tachycardia (heart rate in 130s)\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Leukocyte count: 12.4 ×10³/µL (normal 4.0-11.0 ×10³/µL)- C-reactive protein: 158 mg/L (normal, <8 mg/L)- Urinalysis: negative for leukocyte or nitritesImaging Studies:- Chest radiograph: no consolidationBlood Cultures:- Initial blood culture from tunneled catheter: positive at 7.2 hours- Additional peripheral blood culture: positive at 5.13 hours- Additional catheter blood culture: positive at 8.3 hours- Gram stain from all three sets showed gram-negative bacilliImage Title: Figure 1 - Gram stain and colony appearanceImage Description: Gram stain of positive blood culture bottle at 1000× magnification, with growth appearance on 5% sheep's blood agar plates",
